'''''Teucrium gnaphalodes''''' is a plant species in the genus ''[[Teucrium]]''. It is endemic to the [[Iberian Peninsula]] and grows at altitudes between 200 and 1500 m. It flowers from March to July.

The flavones [[diosmin]], [[cirsimaritin]], [[salvigenin]], [[cirsilineol]], [[cirsiliol]], [[luteolin]], [[apigenin]] and the flavanone [[naringenin]], as well as the flavone glycosides [[luteolin-7-O-β-D-glucoside]] and [[apigenin-7-O-β-D-glucoside]], and the diglycosides [[luteolin-7-O-β-D-rutinoside]], luteolin-7-O-β-D-neohesperidoside ([[veronicastroside]]) and luteolin-7-O-β-D-[[sambubioside]] can be found in ''T. gnaphalodes''.<ref>Flavonoid Aglycones and Glycosides from Teucrium gnaphalodes. F. A. T. Barberán, M. I. Gil, F. Tomás, F. Ferreres and A. Arques, J. Nat. Prod., 1985, 48 (5), pages 859–860, {{doi|10.1021/np50041a040}}</ref>

